[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 475,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 432,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67805,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 192,
        "headingId": 432,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Lymphangioma is a benign vascular lesion composed of a localized collection of dilated lymphatic channels.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:59:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 476,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 433,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67806,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 192,
        "headingId": 433,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>9170/0 Lymphangioma, NOS</p>\r\n<p>9173/0 Cystic lymphangioma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:59:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 477,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 434,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67807,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 192,
        "headingId": 434,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>LA90.12 &amp; XH9MR8 Lymphatic malformations of certain specified sites &amp; Lymphangioma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:59:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 478,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 435,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67808,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 192,
        "headingId": 435,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> cystic hygroma/lymphangioma; lymphatic malformation; cavernous lymphangioma; haemangiolymphangioma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:59:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 479,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 436,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67809,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 192,
        "headingId": 436,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:59:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 480,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 437,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67810,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 192,
        "headingId": 437,
        "headingText": "<p>Lymphangiomas may occur on the chest wall or axilla, and they are relatively rare in the thoracic cavity or lung. Lesions on the face and neck area may extend into the thoracic cavity {25298728}. Lesions can arise in the anterior (prevascular), superior, and posterior (paravertebral) mediastinum {3762227} and the paratracheal, subcarinal, and aortopulmonary window areas {10939497}; &lt;&nbsp;1% of lymphangiomas are purely mediastinal {18435512}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2021-01-04T13:03:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 481,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 438,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67811,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 192,
        "headingId": 438,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Symptoms relate to size and site. Some patients are asymptomatic; others may present with pleural effusion and even respiratory distress.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:59:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 482,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 439,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67812,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 192,
        "headingId": 439,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The age range is wide, but lymphangiomas are usually seen in children and young adults, mainly at birth or during early life {10442614; 4051731; 10936792; 22696733}, with a slight male predominance. Some are associated with Turner syndrome or other malformative syndromes {19591554; 6693110; 6888468}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:59:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 483,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 440,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67813,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 192,
        "headingId": 440,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Congenital or early lesions favour developmental malformations, with genetic abnormalities playing an additional role {18500536}. Somatic mutations in <em>PIK3CA</em> have been reported {25681199}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:59:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 484,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 441,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67814,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 192,
        "headingId": 441,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Lymphangiomas are postulated to occur as a result of abnormal lymphatic system development due to mutations in <em>PIK3CA</em> and other genes involving endothelial growth factor receptor pathways {25681199; 26191320}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:59:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 491,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 448,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67815,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 192,
        "headingId": 448,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Lymphangiomas are multicystic and spongy, and the cavities contain milky or watery chylous fluid.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:59:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 485,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 442,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67816,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 192,
        "headingId": 442,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most are cystic/cavernous, with variably sized thin-walled, dilated lymphatic vessels lined by flattened endothelium and with frequent peripheral lymphocytic aggregates. Lumina may be empty or may contain proteinaceous fluid or lymphocytes. Larger vessels can be surrounded by a smooth muscle layer. Longstanding lesions may show interstitial fibrosis, stromal inflammation, and/or haemosiderin deposition.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:59:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 486,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 443,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67817,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 192,
        "headingId": 443,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:59:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 487,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 444,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67818,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 192,
        "headingId": 444,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:59:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 490,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 447,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67819,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 192,
        "headingId": 447,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em></p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Tumour composed of thin-walled vascular spaces</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em></p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Expression of CD31, ERG, and D2-40</li>\r\n<li>Variable CD34 positivity</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:59:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 488,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 445,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67820,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 192,
        "headingId": 445,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:59:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 489,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 446,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67821,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 192,
        "headingId": 446,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Lymphangiomas are benign, but they may be complex in the thoracic cavity, requiring debulking, repeated drainage, and/or sclerosing procedures {27211980}. Surgical excision can be difficult because of lesional size and extension, with infiltration of mediastinal planes, envelopment of great vessels, and displacement of mediastinal organs {15378120}. Recurrence rates depend on lesion size and depth.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:59:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]